/*
:name: tick_comptime_survives
:type: simulation
*/
// tick + constprop, HALF 1 of 2: the cases that MUST STAY comptime-knowable.
// The opposite half (what must go OPAQUE) is tick_comptime_opaque.prp; read
// them together, they are one specification split by expected verdict.
//
// THE RULES (user ruling 2026-07-25):
//
//   R0  The ITERATION COUNT of a tick is assumed UNKNOWN — always, including
//       when it is written as a literal. A tick count is routinely overridden
//       by a runtime argument, so never optimize on it.
//   R1  A variable updated inside a tick body with anything other than a
//       constant literal is an INDUCTION VARIABLE: assume not-constant, even
//       when it happens to be constant.
//   R2  R0 collapses the rest: since the body may run zero times, a variable
//       written anywhere inside a tick is `prior-value`-or-`written-value`
//       after the loop, which is not knowable. So ANY write inside a tick makes
//       that variable opaque.
//
// What survives is therefore exactly one thing: a variable the tick NEVER
// WRITES. That is what this file pins. It is a short list on purpose — the
// interesting content is the other half.
//
// Failing to fold these is merely conservative rather than wrong, so each is a
// `cassert`: it hard-errors if the value is folded to something WRONG, and
// degrades to a runtime check if it simply could not be proven. The `assert`s
// alongside pin the runtime value independently, so no fixture passes vacuously.
//
// STATUS 2026-07-25: LIVE. `tick`/`step` now lower into LNAST (prp2lnast's
// stmt_dispatch + the `tick` node), and the runner walks a tick body inside an
// uncertain scope, so these fixtures exercise the real barrier. Before that,
// `tick_statement` had no dispatch entry and the whole body was silently dropped
// on the way to LNAST while `prp_sim.cpp`'s CST walk still executed it — which
// is what made a testbench `assert` fold against a stale initializer (lhdsuite
// fixme issue 2).

mod passthru(d:u8) -> (q:u8@[0]) { q = d }

// A1 — a variable the tick never writes is not poisoned by the tick existing.
//
// `untouched` is declared outside and read after the loop; the body never
// assigns it. A tick must not demote it just by being in scope. Without this
// case a "when in doubt, poison everything in scope" implementation would look
// correct.
test passthru.untouched_var_survives {
  mut acc       = passthru
  mut untouched = 5
  mut sink      = 0

  tick 4 {
    acc.d = 3
    sink  = acc.q             // the loop DOES write something, just not `untouched`
    step
  }

  cassert(untouched == 5, "a tick must not poison a variable it never writes")
  assert(untouched == 5, "runtime: untouched")
}

// A3 — a comptime const READ (never written) inside the loop stays comptime.
//
// Reading a comptime value from inside a tick body is not an update, so nothing
// about the loop may demote it — not even the fact that the read happens under
// an unknown iteration count. `from_k` is a separate variable that RECEIVES that
// const inside the loop, so by R2 it is opaque; that half is asserted, not
// casserted, and lives as a contrast here rather than being split across files.
test passthru.comptime_const_read {
  mut acc          = passthru
  comptime const K = 5
  mut from_k       = 0

  tick 4 {
    acc.d  = K                // reading K is not an update -> K stays comptime
    from_k = K                // but writing from_k inside the tick -> R2: opaque
    step
  }

  cassert(K == 5, "a comptime const read inside a tick stays comptime")
  assert(from_k == 5, "runtime: written from a comptime const")
}
